btd - Bluetooth daemon for OpenBSD
==================================

btd drives a USB Bluetooth controller attached as ugen(4) from
userland.  It keeps Bluetooth Low Energy keyboards and mice connected
and injects their input into wscons(4), and plays the sndiod(8) mix
on an A2DP headset, with AVRCP absolute volume tied to output.level.

The daemon is split into privilege separated processes:

  main      configuration, firmware, bonds, control socket setup
  bus       USB control requests, interrupt and bulk endpoints,
            wscons injection; no pledge(2), unveil(2) only
  engine    HCI, L2CAP, SMP, ATT/GATT, HOGP, HID, SDP, AVDTP, AVRCP,
            RTP; chroot(2), pledge "stdio"
  frontend  control socket for btctl(8), sndio recording and SBC
            encoding, mixer volume

Supported hardware: Realtek RTL8761BU (TP-Link UB500).  Its firmware
is loaded from /etc/firmware/rtl8761bu_fw and rtl8761bu_config.

Build and install:

	make obj
	make
	doas make install
	doas install -m 555 etc/rc.d/btd /etc/rc.d/btd

Run the tests (the SBC test needs ffmpeg to decode):

	make regress

Setup:

	doas useradd -L daemon -g =uid -s /sbin/nologin -d /var/empty \
	    -c "Bluetooth Daemon" _btd
	doas install -d -m 755 /var/db/btd
	doas install -d -m 700 -o _btd -g _btd /var/db/btd/.sndio
	doas install -m 600 -o _btd -g _btd ~/.sndio/cookie \
	    /var/db/btd/.sndio/cookie

Pairing:

List the devices in /etc/btd.conf and put each one in pairing mode;
btd pairs with a configured device that is not bonded yet when it
finds it.  A keyboard asks for a passkey, shown by "btctl show" and
in the log.  Bonds are kept in /var/db/btd/bonds.

Some LE devices, such as Logitech keyboards and mice, advertise a new
address each time they enter pairing mode.  To pair one again, update
its hid line to the new address and run "btctl reload".

Keyboard LEDs:

Keyboard LEDs such as Caps Lock follow the state X sets on wskbd0,
polled every 200 ms.  Without a keyboard attached as wskbd0 they stay
off.

Dropped audio packets are reported in the log every 10 seconds while
they occur; with -v, btd also logs when packets start and stop being
dropped and how long ago the controller last completed one.

See btd(8), btd.conf(5) and btctl(8).
